# Keystone PM — configuration (R-007). Copy to .env.local (development) or set the variables in the hosting platform.
# Every variable is optional unless marked REQUIRED; an empty value counts as unset. The values are validated at start-up
# (src/server/config/parse.ts): in production (NODE_ENV=production) an invalid value, or a missing required one, stops the
# server with one log line naming the variable (never its value); in development the problem is logged and the default used.
# Never commit a real secret: .env* files are git-ignored except this one. In production keep the secrets (API keys,
# database password) in the platform's secret store.

# ── Runtime ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Set by Next.js: development (`next dev`), production (`next build` / `next start`, the Mac app), test (Vitest).
# NODE_ENV=production
# `next start` listening address (Keystone uses 3000 on the Mac; Codex 3100/3110, Vigie 3200, Cursus 3210/3217/5173).
# PORT=3000
# HOSTNAME=0.0.0.0

# ── Database (R-002) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# PostgreSQL server. When set, Keystone uses a node-postgres connection pool; migrations and the first seed run at deploy
# time with `npm run db:migrate` (never at start-up), and the server refuses to start while a migration is pending.
# SSL and other connection options go in the URL (e.g. ?sslmode=require). Unset: embedded PGlite in KEYSTONE_DATA_DIR/pg
# (one server process; migrated and seeded on first use).
# DATABASE_URL=postgresql://keystone:change-me@db.example.internal:5432/keystone?sslmode=require
# Connections per server process (1–200, default 10). Ignored without DATABASE_URL.
# DATABASE_POOL_MAX=10

# Data directory: PGlite database (<dir>/pg, without DATABASE_URL), the files Keystone stores (<dir>/files: task
# attachments, photos, meeting transcripts, imports) and the antivirus quarantine (<dir>/quarantine). Default ./.data.
# The Mac app uses ~/Library/Application Support/Keystone/data.
# KEYSTONE_DATA_DIR=./.data

# ── Backups (R-004, I-1b: RPO ≤ 15 min, RTO ≤ 4 h) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# OFF unless this folder is set (the Mac app and development never write snapshots by themselves). Every snapshot goes to
# <dir>/<UTC stamp>/ (database dump + manifest.json); the stored files are copied once into <dir>/content/ (by sha256).
# Must be outside <data dir>/files and <data dir>/pg. The RPO is met only once this folder is replicated OFF the host (I-1a).
# The "backup" job takes the snapshots: `npm run worker` with PostgreSQL (pg_dump ≥ the server's major version on its PATH),
# KEYSTONE_JOBS=inprocess with PGlite. By hand: `npm run backup`; restore: `npm run restore -- <snapshot> --target …`.
# KEYSTONE_BACKUP_DIR=/backups/keystone
# Minutes between two snapshots (5–1440, default 10; above 15 the 15-minute RPO cannot be met).
# KEYSTONE_BACKUP_EVERY_MIN=10
# Retention: every snapshot for <h> hours, then the newest of each day for <d> days, then of each month for <m> months.
# KEYSTONE_BACKUP_RETENTION=48h,30d,12m

# ── Antivirus on uploaded files (R-175, platform default — owner and IT to confirm) ────────────────────────────────────
# clamav: every upload (task attachments, photos, meeting transcripts, imports) is checked by a clamd service BEFORE it is
# stored; an infected file is refused (HTTP 422) and kept 30 days in <data dir>/quarantine for IT; while clamd does not
# answer every upload is refused (HTTP 503, fail-closed). A daily job rescans recent and never-scanned files; a stored file
# it flags is blocked (downloads answer 410) and kept until IT acts. off: no check.
# REQUIRED with KEYSTONE_SEED=production (clamav, or off explicitly); unset elsewhere in production → a warning; default off.
# KEYSTONE_FILE_SCAN=clamav
# clamd's address: TCP host and port (no default port — clamd's usual 3310 is Keystone's smoke-test port on the Mac, never
# run clamd there), or its unix socket. Settings for IT (StreamMaxLength ≥ 21M, AlertExceedsMax…): docs/EXPLOITATION.md.
# CLAMAV_HOST=clamav
# CLAMAV_PORT=3310
# CLAMAV_SOCKET=/run/clamav/clamd.ctl

# ── Seed of a new database (R-011, decision A-15) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# demo (default): the prototype data set (17 projects, HLT-026…) for development, tests and demonstrations.
# production: reference data only (lifecycle stages, calendars, Settings lists, document and machine types, departments and
# zones without people) — no project, no person, no record of the prototype.
# Only an EMPTY database is seeded; an existing one keeps the data set it was created with. With DATABASE_URL,
# `npm run db:migrate` refuses to seed an empty database unless KEYSTONE_SEED is set explicitly.
# KEYSTONE_SEED=production

# ── Business clock (R-013) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The date Keystone works at: lateness, due dates, the dates stamped on records, form defaults, the portfolio "now" line.
# Unset (recommended) or real: the real date (Europe/Paris), on every database, the demo one included (owner, 17 Oct 2026).
# YYYY-MM-DD: a pinned date (demonstrations of the prototype data set at its own date — 2026-09-25 —, reproducible
# screenshots; the time of day stays real). The tests pin 2026-09-25. A pinned date in production logs a warning at start-up.
# KEYSTONE_CLOCK=real

# ── Sign-in (R-038, decision I-2a) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# demo (default): the "View as" persona switcher, no sign-in (development, tests, demonstrations).
# entra: every page needs a Microsoft Entra ID session; a user signs in when their e-mail is on a person in Keystone and the
# token carries a Keystone app role (or a group mapped below). Any other value is a configuration error.
# KEYSTONE_AUTH=entra
# REQUIRED with entra: at least 32 characters; encrypts the session and sign-in cookies (keep it in the secret store).
# KEYSTONE_SESSION_SECRET=
# Session lifetime in hours (1–168, default 12; absolute, no sliding renewal).
# KEYSTONE_SESSION_HOURS=12
# REQUIRED with entra: the tenant (directory) id, or ENTRA_ISSUER for another authority.
# ENTRA_TENANT_ID=00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000
# ENTRA_ISSUER=https://login.microsoftonline.com/<tenant>/v2.0
# REQUIRED with entra: the Keystone app registration (confidential client) and its Web redirect URI.
# ENTRA_CLIENT_ID=
# ENTRA_CLIENT_SECRET=
# ENTRA_REDIRECT_URI=https://keystone.example.internal/api/auth/callback
# Where Entra sends the browser after sign-out (default <origin of the redirect URI>/sign-in?signedOut=1).
# ENTRA_POST_LOGOUT_REDIRECT_URI=
# Entra groups granting Keystone roles, besides the app roles: {"<group object id>": "pm" | ["pm", "englead"]}.
# Role values: director, pm, depthead, englead, quality, procurement, finance, member, client (app roles may be prefixed
# "Keystone.", e.g. Keystone.Finance). For IT (R-044): the Finance role needs the app role "Keystone.Finance" (value
# "finance") on the app registration, or a group mapped here to "finance"; until then no real user holds it.
# ENTRA_GROUP_ROLES=

# ── Scheduled jobs (R-006) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Outbox deliveries (Vigie, Cursus, Codex), purges and file pruning. With PostgreSQL run `npm run worker` beside the web
# servers. inprocess: the web server runs them itself (the way with PGlite, which one process only can open).
# KEYSTONE_JOBS=inprocess
# exclusive: lets `npm run worker` open the PGlite database — only while the web server is stopped (maintenance run).
# KEYSTONE_WORKER_PGLITE=exclusive
# Hour of the automatic reminders (R-121, owner's answer of 17 Oct 2026: « les alertes automatiques de rappel doivent arriver
# le matin »), HH:MM Europe/Paris, default 07:00. The jobs that send reminders to people (today: site-prerequisite-alerts) run
# once a day at that hour; a worker started later in the day runs the day's missed run at once, never twice the same day.
# KEYSTONE_REMINDERS_AT=07:00

# ── Logs (R-009) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# debug | info (default) | warn | error | silent
# LOG_LEVEL=info
# json (default in production: one JSON object per line on stdout/stderr) | pretty (default otherwise)
# LOG_FORMAT=json
# Health check for the hosting platform: GET /api/health → 200 when the database answers and has no pending migration, else 503.

# ── Sibling applications: links (inlined in the browser bundles at BUILD time — set them before `npm run build`) ───────
# NEXT_PUBLIC_CODEX_URL=https://codex.example.internal      # default http://localhost:3100
# NEXT_PUBLIC_CURSUS_URL=https://cursus.example.internal    # default http://localhost:5173
# NEXT_PUBLIC_VIGIE_URL=https://vigie.example.internal      # default http://localhost:3200

# ── Cursus connector (people's site clearances, Go / No Go, travel requests) ────────────────────────────────────────────
# API base URL. Unset: the mock connector over the local mirror (nothing leaves Keystone).
# CURSUS_API_URL=https://cursus.example.internal
# Key sent as X-Api-Key (Cursus checks it against its own KEYSTONE_API_KEY). REQUIRED in production when CURSUS_API_URL is
# set; the Cursus demo key "demo-keystone" is the default outside production only and is refused in production — except for
# a Cursus on the same machine (localhost / 127.x / ::1 URL, e.g. the Mac app next to a local Cursus), where it is a warning.
# CURSUS_API_KEY=
# Accepted instead of CURSUS_API_KEY (same value as Cursus's variable name).
# KEYSTONE_API_KEY=
# Key Cursus presents (X-Api-Key) to Keystone's events endpoint POST /api/integrations/cursus/events (R-029, NEW-CURSUS-7;
# docs/integrations/cursus-contract.md). A SEPARATE secret (never CURSUS_API_KEY, which Keystone sends to Cursus), at least
# 16 characters. Outside production the demo key "demo-cursus-events" is the default; production refuses it (start-up
# error) and, without a key, the endpoint answers 503 to every call (Cursus keeps its events and retries).
# CURSUS_EVENTS_KEY=
# on: import Cursus's staff directory (matricule, name, e-mail, team) daily and from "Sync now" (R-027, NEW-CURSUS-2).
# Off by default — Cursus has no directory endpoint yet; switch it on once it exists (on | off).
# CURSUS_DIRECTORY=off

# ── Vigie connector (ECOs, NCRs, site HSE) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# API base URL. Unset: the mock connectors over the local mirrors. The endpoints are Keystone's proposal until Vigie
# publishes its API (open question V-2).
# VIGIE_API_URL=https://vigie.example.internal
# Sent as X-Api-Key when set. Provisional: how Vigie authenticates Keystone is open question V-3; production logs a warning
# while VIGIE_API_URL is set without it.
# VIGIE_API_KEY=

# ── Codex connector (document approvals, R-124) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# API base URL. Unset: the mock connector — approved releases stay "Pending sync with Codex" (nothing leaves Keystone).
# Set: the approval outbox is delivered to Codex (endpoints are Keystone's proposal until Codex publishes its API, D-1).
# CODEX_API_URL=https://codex.example.internal

# ── Connectors configured by IT (R-020, D-7a) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# JSON file holding every connector's settings (enabled, frequency, direction, data scope, owner — never credentials; see
# config/connectors.example.json). Applied when the connectors are first read and whenever the file changes; a file with
# any error is not applied. Check a file before deploying it: npx tsx scripts/connectors-check.ts <file>.
# Unset: the stored settings stay (the initial data, or the last file applied).
# KEYSTONE_CONNECTORS_FILE=/etc/keystone/connectors.json

# ── Meeting transcript analysis by Claude (optional, S-10) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Only when this key is set is a transcript sent to Anthropic for analysis (the meeting screen says so). Unset: the local
# rule-based analyser runs and nothing leaves Keystone.
# GDPR (R-173): Anthropic then processes personal data for EPC2i — sign the sub-processing agreement (DPA) first. Production
# (or KEYSTONE_SEED=production) logs a reminder at start-up while the key is set and KEYSTONE_AI is not off.
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
# off disables Claude even with a key (on | off).
# KEYSTONE_AI=on
# Model id (default claude-opus-5-5).
# KEYSTONE_AI_MODEL=claude-opus-5-5
# off drops the server-side refusal fallback (for a model that does not accept it) (on | off).
# KEYSTONE_AI_FALLBACKS=on
# Tests only: 1 lets a test enable Claude (tests mock the SDK and never reach the network).
# KEYSTONE_AI_ALLOW_IN_TESTS=

# ── Build and development tools ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Output folder of `next build` (default .next; CI and verification builds use e.g. .next-ci).
# KEYSTONE_DIST_DIR=.next-ci
# 1 shows the component gallery (/dev/components) in a production build.
# KS_DEV_GALLERY=
# NEXT_TELEMETRY_DISABLED=1
# Turbopack workspace root, for a build inside a git worktree whose node_modules is a symlink (agent worktrees only).
# KEYSTONE_TURBOPACK_ROOT=
# Port of the server the end-to-end suite starts (`npm run test:e2e`; default: a free port, never 3000, 3100, 3110, 3200,
# 3210, 3217, 3310 or 5173).
# E2E_PORT=
